doc/ui-cascade-spec:
cascade specification ui  ui contributor shared/libs/ui  technical details of how the theme cascade translates to css. for the conceptual model, see architecture (`architecture.md`). selector model  two attributes, never on the same node: <div element="surface--card">        element = {family}--{element}
<div layout="left-sidebar">          layout = bare name (no prefix) html selector model › part attribute rules: only emit css for `part` values **explicitly declared** in the theme under that surface's `parts` field. an undeclared `part` is inert — zero css emitted. this prevents developers from using `part` as a free styling escape hatch. `part` is never used on the same node as `element` or `layout`. selector emitted: `[element="surface--card"] [part="body"] { ... }` `part="name"` marks a structural sub-region of a themed ancestor. use it on plain divs that a designer needs to control (padding, gap, background) without giving the div a full element identity. <div element="surface--card">
  <div part="body">      ← designer controls interior here
  <div part="actions">   ← designer controls gap here html selector model › family prefixes (for element attribute)  ascii | family key              | prefix          |
|-------------------------|-----------------|
| surfacecontainer        | `surface`       |
| elevatedoverlay         | `overlay`       |
| interactivecontrol      | `control`       |
| interactivecontrolgroup | `control-group` |
| field                   | `field`         |
| listcontainer           | `list`          |
| shape                   | `shape`         |
| media                   | `media`         | layout uses `layout="value"` directly — no family prefix. selector model › axis attributes (bare, no data- prefix)  `emphasis`, `status`, `state`, `scale`, `concept` css specificity  css source order + specificity handles cascade priority. no variable chains or bridge systems. the engine emits rules in this order: [element^="surface--"]                                  l2 family (lowest)
[element="surface--card"]                               l3 element
[element="surface--card"][emphasis="primary"]            axis (emphasis first)
[element="surface--card"][status="error"]                axis (status later = wins)
[element="surface--card"]:hover                         pseudo-class (highest)
[element="surface--card"][emphasis="primary"]:hover      compound (most specific)
[concept="x"] [element="surface--card"]                 concept (2 selectors)
[element="a"] [element="b"]                             contextual (2 selectors) composition variables everything else is plain css. padding, radius, shadow, cursor, transition, typography — set directly, no variables. 5 css custom properties handle cross-property composition: ascii | variable | why it exists |
|----------|---------------|
| `--bg` | tint mixes into background via `color-mix()` |
| `--fg` | `smart-bw` computes foreground from background |
| `--tint` | one color modifies both background and border |
| `--border-color` | tint mixes into border via `color-mix()` |
| `--bg-alpha` | alpha composites with transparent | composition variables › property application (on [element] and [layout])  [element] {
  background: color-mix(in srgb,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint, var(--bg, transparent)) 20%, var(--bg, transparent))
    var(--bg-alpha, 100%),
    transparent
  );
  color: var(--fg, inherit);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb,
    var(--tint, var(--border-color, transparent)) 30%,
    var(--border-color, transparent)
  );
} css output principles **zero theme = zero cascade lines.** only structural plumbing remains. **only emit what the theme defines.** no defaults copied to elements. **deltas only.** elements don't repeat family values. **one file, all modes.** default on `:root`, others on `[data-mode]`. **readable in devtools.** each rule shows what it does. smart bleed css inner radius = outer radius - border-width (child sits inside parent border). for `border` mode, child extends over the border, so outer radius is used. for `rounded` mode, interior edges also get radius. resolved at build time. the engine computes the parent's padding, radius, and border-width from the cascade, then emits: /* horizontal bleed into parent padding */
[selector] {
  margin-left: calc(-1 * <parent-px>);
  margin-right: calc(-1 * <parent-px>);
  padding-left: <parent-px>;
  padding-right: <parent-px>;
}

/* first child: bleed top + inherit parent top corners */
[selector]:first-child {
  margin-top: calc(-1 * <parent-py>);
  padding-top: <parent-py>;
  border-top-left-radius: calc(<parent-radius-tl> - <parent-border-width>);
  border-top-right-radius: calc(<parent-radius-tr> - <parent-border-width>);
  border-bottom-left-radius: 0;   /* straight interior edge (normal mode) */
  border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

/* last child: bleed bottom + inherit parent bottom corners */
[selector]:last-child { ... } css unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/ui-theme-authoring:
theme authoring ui  ui consumer shared/libs/ui  how to write a theme object. a theme is a typescript object conforming to the `theme` type (`shared/libs/ui/types/theme.ts`). theme object structure  theme
├── id, name
│
├── colors                         l1 primitive
│   ├── main (white, black)
│   ├── brand (1–4)
│   ├── status (success, info, warning, error → can reference flat)
│   ├── flat (11 accent colors)
│   └── semantictokens (primary, secondary, accent, muted)
│
├── typography                     l1 primitive
│   ├── fonts (primary, secondary, quote, monoprimary, monosecondary)
│   └── types (18 scales, each: fontfamily, fontstyle, fontsizemobile,
│              fontsizedesktop, fontweight, lineheight, letterspacing)
│
├── spacing                        l1 primitive (unit multiplier → xs..3xl)
├── radius                         l1 primitive (none, xs, sm, md, lg, xl, full)
├── effects                        l1 primitive (named effect presets)
├── transitions                    l1 primitive (duration + timing scales)
├── borderwidths                   l1 primitive (none → extrathick)
├── icons                          l1 primitive (set, sizes, weight)
│
├── modes                          cross-cutting
│   └── colorscheme
│       └── each mode: background + foreground anchors + derivation params
│
├── families                       l2 — morphological family defaults
│   ├── surfacecontainer           (is the base shape)
│   ├── elevatedoverlay            (surface + overlay extras)
│   ├── listcontainer              (surface + row/cell/separator/alternating)
│   ├── interactivecontrol         (surface wrapper)
│   ├── interactivecontrolgroup    (surface wrapper)
│   ├── field                      (surface + placeholder/caret/obfuscation)
│   ├── shape                      (surface wrapper)
│   └── media                      (surface + objectfit/aspectratio/overlay/filter)
│
├── elements                       l3 — element overrides (sparse deltas)
│
├── concepts                       l4 — product-specific semantic overrides
│   └── [conceptname]: { [target]: surfacecontainertokens + accent + contextual }
│
└── contextual                     l5 — parent → child overrides
    └── [parent]: { [child]: surfacecontainertokens }
        keys are element names (e.g. 'sidebar', 'header', 'card')
        matching the keys in elementtypes. not the full attribute
        value (not 'surface--sidebar'). theme object structure › what the tree leaves out beside the levels above, a theme carries: `version` (the folder is `v<version>`), `type` (`wireframe` | `high-fidelity`), `kind` (`seed` | `product`). `platform`: absent means web, `"mobile"` means mobile. a theme is for one platform; see mobile. `gradients`: named gradient definitions, referenced as `gradient-<name>` in a `background`. `icons`: the icon set, the five icon sizes in px, the stroke weight. `layouts`: the regions of a page, themed like elements; see layouts. `rules` (l6): cascade chains deeper than parent → child; see concepts, contextual, rules. `press` (mobile only): what a touch shows. the surface every family, element and region is written with the same token shape, `surfacecontainertokens`. each key is optional; write only what the level above does not already say. `boundary`: `border` (width, style, colour, per side), `radius` (all, per corner), `effect` (a named preset or layers), `outline` (web only), `margin`. `interior`: `background` (a colour or a gradient), `backgroundalpha`, `foreground`, `opacity`, `padding` (all, x, y, per side), `gap` (all, h, v), `separator`, `childalign` (horizontal, vertical), `scrollbar` (web only). `dimensionposition`: `width`, `height`, `translatex`, `translatey`. `defaulttypography`: `typographytype` (one of the 18 type scales), `textalign`, `verticalalign`, `texttransform`, `textdecoration`, `texteffect`. it never carries colour: colour is `interior.foreground`. `tint`: the accent colour the pattern reads through `'tint'`. `cursor` (web only), `smartbleed` (`false` | `normal` | `rounded` | `border` | `border-rounded`), `transition` (duration, timing). `combined`: the entries per axis value, see the axes. the axes an element's look varies along axes. the markup carries the axis as an attribute; the theme writes what changes for each value. `shared/libs/ui/types/axes.ts` holds the values, `shared/libs/ui/types/elements.ts` says which axes each element takes. an element not listed for an axis ignores that axis. `concept` is on every element and is never listed. `emphasis`, `status`, `state` and `position` are written as `combined` entries on a surface: `{ where: { <axis>: <value> }, partialsurface: {…} }`. a `where` with several keys is a combination. the engine emits the `combined` entries in the order they are written. write emphasis, then status, then state, then the combinations, so the later ones win. `scale` is not a `combined` key: it is authored in `sizes`, see size variants. emphasis carries the pattern; status carries the colour over it; state carries the delta while it lasts. see tint pattern. ascii | axis      | values                                                        | carries                                   | written as              |
|-----------|---------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------------|
| emphasis  | hero, primary, secondary, tertiary, quiet                     | the pattern: filled, outlined, ghost      | where: { emphasis }     |
| status    | neutral, info, success, warning, error, destructive           | the tint over the pattern                 | where: { status }       |
| state     | hover, active, focus, selected, disabled, loading             | the delta while the state lasts           | where: { state }        |
|           | mobile: no hover; long-pressed in its place; swiped on rows   |                                           |                         |
| position  | first, last, only, middle, even, odd (sibling order)          | edges and separators of a row in a list   | where: { position }     |
| scale     | 3xs 2xs xs sm md lg xl 2xl 3xl (default md)                   | padding, typography, icon size; no colour | sizes: { md: {…} }      |
| concept   | a free name                                                   | a product meaning                         | concepts (l4)           | interactivecontrol: {
  surface: {
    interior: { background: "background", foreground: "foreground" },
    boundary: { border: { width: "thin", style: "solid", color: "gray-300" } },
    combined: [
      { where: { emphasis: "primary" }, partialsurface: { tint: "primary", interior: { background: "tint", foreground: "smart-bw" }, boundary: { border: { color: "transparent" } } } },
      { where: { emphasis: "quiet" }, partialsurface: { interior: { background: "transparent", foreground: "gray-600" }, boundary: { border: { color: "transparent" } } } },
      { where: { status: "error" }, partialsurface: { tint: "status-error", interior: { foreground: "status-error" }, boundary: { border: { color: "status-error" } } } },
      { where: { emphasis: "primary", status: "error" }, partialsurface: { interior: { background: "tint", foreground: "smart-bw" } } },
      { where: { state: "hover" }, partialsurface: { boundary: { border: { color: "gray-500" } } } },
      { where: { emphasis: "primary", state: "hover" }, partialsurface: { interior: { background: "brand-1-dark" } } },
      { where: { state: "focus" }, partialsurface: { boundary: { outline: { width: "thin", style: "solid", color: "brand-1", offset: "1px" } } } },
      { where: { state: "disabled" }, partialsurface: { interior: { opacity: 0.45 }, cursor: "not-allowed" } },
    ],
  },
} typescript a control: the base, two emphases, a status, a combination, and the states. families eight families. every element belongs to one; the family block is where most of a theme is written. `surfacecontainer` is the base every other family inherits; the others add the tokens below beside their `surface`. `surfacecontainer` (`surface--…`): card, panel, section, header, footer, badge, avatar, tile, alert, separator, spinner, skeleton, progress… written bare: its tokens are the surface itself. `elevatedoverlay` (`overlay--…`): dialog, drawer, popover, tooltip, toast, sheet, menu. adds `overlay`: `scrimcolor`, `scrimopacity`, `scrimblur`, `entranceanimation`, `exitanimation`. `interactivecontrol` (`control--…`): button, icon-button, chip, toggle, checkbox, radio, tab-trigger, sidebar-item… adds `starticon`, `endicon` (each a surface). `switch` has its own shape: `track` and `thumb`. `interactivecontrolgroup` (`control-group--…`): tab-bar, breadcrumb, pagination, button-group, toggle-group, sidebar. a `surface` only. `field` (`field--…`): input, textarea, select, combobox, date-picker, otp, phone… adds `starticon`, `endicon`, `placeholdercolor`, `placeholderopacity`, `caretcolor`, `obfuscation` (character, letterspacing, fontsize). `listcontainer` (`list--…`): list, accordion, menu, tree. adds `row`, `cell`, `alternating` (surfaces) and `separator` (a surface with `height`, `width`). `table` elements (`table--…`): root, header, body, row, cell, footer. a surface each; `tablerow` and `tablefootercell` add `alternating`. `shape` (`shape--…`): icon, logo, decorative. a `surface` only. `media` (`media--…`): image, video, embed. adds `objectfit`, `objectposition`, `aspectratio`, `overlay` (colour, opacity, blur), `filter` (grayscale, brightness, contrast, blur). the elements of each family, their axes and their platform are the rows of `shared/libs/ui/types/elements.ts`; the names people write are in the markup dictionary. layouts the regions of a page are themed in `layouts`, keyed by the camelcase region name (`pageheader` → `layout="page-header"`). a region carries a `surface` and the dimensions the theme owns. `layouts.surface` is the default of every region, as `families.surfacecontainer` is for elements. headers (`pageheader`, `secondpageheader`, `mainheader`, `secondmainheader`, `mainfooter`): `height`, and `sm`/`md`/`lg` heights for `scale`. sidebars (`leftsidebar`, `rightsidebar`, `contextpanel`, `shellnav`): `width`, and `sm`/`md`/`lg` widths. `leftsidebar` adds `widthcollapsed` and a `collapsed` surface. `container`: `maxwidth` per scale `xs`…`xl`. `dialog`: `maxwidth` per `sm`/`md`/`lg`. `sheet`: `size` per `sm`/`md`/`lg`/`full`. their visual tokens stay in `elements`. `scrollarea`: the `scrollbar` only. `layouts.emphasis` has two values, `primary` and `secondary`: a region's shade over its own base, for a region carrying `emphasis`. the region's own rule keeps its border edge. regions do not take `status`, `state` or `combined`. layouts: {
  surface: { interior: { background: "background" } },
  pageheader: {
    height: "56px", sm: { height: "48px" }, md: { height: "56px" }, lg: { height: "72px" },
    surface: { interior: { background: "brand-2", foreground: "smart-bw", padding: { x: "xl" } } },
  },
  leftsidebar: {
    width: "240px", sm: { width: "200px" }, md: { width: "240px" }, lg: { width: "300px" },
    surface: { boundary: { border: { right: { width: "thin", style: "solid", color: "gray-200" } } } },
  },
  mainbody: { surface: { interior: { background: "gray-100", padding: { x: "xl", y: "lg" } } } },
  emphasis: {
    primary: { surface: { interior: { background: "brand-2", foreground: "smart-bw" } } },
    secondary: { surface: { interior: { background: "gray-100", foreground: "foreground" } } },
  },
  container: { sm: { maxwidth: "640px" }, md: { maxwidth: "940px" }, lg: { maxwidth: "1340px" } },
  dialog: { sm: { maxwidth: "480px" }, md: { maxwidth: "640px" }, lg: { maxwidth: "940px" } },
  scrollarea: { scrollbar: { thumbcolor: "gray-300", trackcolor: "transparent", width: "8px" } },
} typescript color references derived colours the engine computes: `gray-50`…`gray-950` (from the mode's background and foreground), `gray-pure-<step>` (mode independent), `brand-<n>-light` | `brand-<n>-dark`, `brand-<n>-gray-<step>` (a brand-tinted gray), `status-<x>-light` | `status-<x>-dark`, `smart-bw`, `tint`. the amounts are the mode's `derivation`. a `background` may be a gradient: `gradient-<name>`, defined in `gradients` as one layer or a stack of layers (linear, radial, conic). theme token values can reference colors by name: ascii | type | examples |
|------|----------|
| primitive | `'main-white'`, `'brand-1'`, `'status-error'`, `'flat-blue'` |
| derived | `'gray-200'`, `'brand-1-light'`, `'status-error-dark'` |
| semantic | `'primary'`, `'secondary'`, `'accent'`, `'muted'` |
| mode anchors | `'background'`, `'foreground'` (resolved from active color mode, not semantic tokens) |
| special | `'tint'` (current tint value), `'smart-bw'` (auto black/white) |
| css | `'transparent'`, `'currentcolor'`, `'inherit'` |
| raw hex | `'#ff0000'` (escape hatch, avoid when possible) | color references › which colour to write a surface's fill is `'background'`; its text is `'foreground'`. the two swap in dark mode. text on a coloured fill (a brand, a status) is `'smart-bw'`. `'main-white'` and `'main-black'` are written only where the colour must not swap: the mode anchors and the shadows. a tinted ground is a brand-tinted gray (`'brand-1-gray-50'`), with the amount in the mode's `graycolormixpercentage`. modes  each color mode defines background/foreground anchors and derivation parameters. the engine computes gray scales, brand light/dark, status light/dark from these. modes: {
  colorscheme: {
    light: {
      background: 'main-white',
      foreground: 'main-black',
      derivation: {
        brandlightamount: 74,
        branddarkamount: 20,
        statuslightamount: 74,
        statusdarkamount: 20,
        graycolormixpercentage: '5%',
        flatsaturationadjust: 0,
      },
    },
    dark: { ... },
  },
} typescript concepts concepts apply to both elements and layouts. `concept="dashboard"` on a shell → the theme overrides `maincontent` background for dashboard pages. group overrides by business meaning, not by element type: concepts: {
  knowledge: {
    card: {
      accent: 'brand-2',
      boundary: { border: { width: 'default', color: 'gray-pure-500' } },
      contextual: {
        header: {
          smartbleed: 'normal',
          interior: { background: 'brand-2', foreground: 'smart-bw' },
        },
      },
    },
  },
  dashboard: {
    maincontent: {
      interior: { background: 'gray-50' },
    },
  },
} typescript contextual overrides and rules two more levels, for what a family or an element cannot say on its own. l5 `contextual`: parent → child, keyed by element names (`sidebar: { button: {…} }`), the child typed as its own family shape. for a child that looks different inside one parent. l6 `rules`: a `chain` of segments, each `{ key, concept? }` (an element, a layout or a family name, with the concept the node carries), and the `tokens` to apply at its end. for anything deeper than parent → child, or concepts on several nodes of the chain. neither repairs what a family should have set. an override written here to fix a general look is a family value in the wrong place. contextual: {
  sidebar: { button: { surface: { interior: { background: "transparent" } } } },
},
rules: [
  { chain: [{ key: "page", concept: "special" }, { key: "card", concept: "knowledge" }, { key: "header" }], tokens: { interior: { background: "brand-2", foreground: "smart-bw" } } },
] typescript size variants default scale when absent: `md`. the scale values are `3xs 2xs xs sm md lg xl 2xl 3xl`; a size entry is a `partialsurface` and an `icon` size. scale changes padding, typography and icon size, never colour. it is not a `combined` key. elements the table gives a `scale` define their sizes in `sizes`, keyed by scale. a family may author `sizes` too: every element of the family inherits them, an element overrides with its own. families: {
  interactivecontrol: {
    sizes: {
      sm: { partialsurface: { interior: { padding: { x: "sm", y: "xs" } }, defaulttypography: { typographytype: "bodysmall" } }, icon: { size: "xs" } },
      md: { partialsurface: { interior: { padding: { x: "md", y: "sm" } }, defaulttypography: { typographytype: "body" } }, icon: { size: "sm" } },
    },
  },
},
elements: {
  iconbutton: { sizes: { md: { partialsurface: { interior: { padding: { all: "sm" } } } } } },   // only what differs from the family
} typescript tint pattern  emphasis defines the pattern and sets `tint`; status overrides `tint` only, so the pattern keeps its shape and changes colour. both are written as `combined` entries, see the axes. interactivecontrol: {
  surface: {
    combined: [
      // emphasis = the pattern; tint = the colour
      { where: { emphasis: "primary" }, partialsurface: { tint: "primary", interior: { background: "tint", foreground: "smart-bw" }, boundary: { border: { color: "transparent" } } } },
      // status re-tints: the pattern stays, the colour changes
      { where: { status: "error" }, partialsurface: { tint: "status-error" } },
      // filled + status: the fill takes the status colour
      { where: { emphasis: "primary", status: "error" }, partialsurface: { interior: { background: "tint", foreground: "smart-bw" } } },
    ],
  },
} typescript mobile a mobile theme is the same type with `platform: "mobile"`: the same core, families, elements and cascade, in mobile's shapes, plus what only a phone has. no `hover`, `cursor`, `scrollbar` or `outline`. writing one in a mobile theme does not compile. states: the shared ones (`active`, `focus`, `selected`, `disabled`, `loading`) plus `long-pressed` (in the place of `hover`) and `swiped` (a row slid sideways). the elements table says which an element takes (`mobilestate`). regions in `layouts`: `screen`, `appbar`, `body` (the one that scrolls), `actionbar`, `tabbar`, `drawer` (with `width`), each with a `surface` and `safearea` edges; bars have `height` with `sm`/`md`/`lg`; `dialog` and `sheet` sizes. `press`: `opacitydim` (ios) and `ripple` (android: `color`, `borderless`). a mobile theme addresses only the elements the table gives to mobile (`platform`). concepts, contextual, rules and gradients are not emitted by the mobile engine; the build lists them as `unsupported`. a product's web and mobile apps wear two themes. to share values, the mobile theme imports them from the web theme (`colors: webtheme.colors`). build and activate a theme compiles to one file per platform; a project points at one theme per platform. `pnpm theme:build` builds every theme: a web theme to `themes/<id>/v<n>/generated/<id>.css`, a mobile theme to `generated/<id>.native.json`. a project names its themes in `shared/themes/active.json`: the web theme at the top, the mobile theme under `mobile`. ux/ui → theme sets each. a design never names a theme. `active.json` is the one pointer. the design system is the test: walk foundations, components, patterns, layouts and the check pages after each level, in light and in dark. unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed
